This is also a question type in IELTS reading, which we are exploring today.

13
00:02:03,830 --> 00:02:05,330
Matching Sentence Endings.

14
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:08,550
Our lesson has three parts.

15
00:02:09,190 --> 00:02:14,450
1. Introduction to Matching Sentence Endings

16
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:18,950
2. Steps for Matching Sentence Endings

17
00:02:19,790 --> 00:02:20,490
 3. Practice

18
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:27,110
This lesson aims to help you understand features of Matching Sentence Endings.

19
00:02:27,830 --> 00:02:31,090
Understand and apply steps for Matching Sentence Endings.

20
00:02:32,090 --> 00:02:33,110
Let's get started.

21
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:41,480
Matching Sentence Endings is a question type that requires you to match

22
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:46,740
incomplete sentences with no ending with their sentence endings, to form

23
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:50,280
complete sentences that carry the exact information of the passage.

24
00:02:51,260 --> 00:02:53,960
Matching Sentence Endings has the following parts.

25
00:02:56,220 --> 00:03:05,540
Requirements Numbered questions or incomplete
sentences Sentence Endings are lettered A, B, C, etc.

26
00:03:06,490 --> 00:03:11,480
The purpose of this question type is to check
your ability to connect the information together.

27
00:03:12,240 --> 00:03:16,080
This question type is quite difficult because it has the following features.

28
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:21,100
The number of sentence endings is more than the number of questions, which

29
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:23,300
makes it quite difficult for candidates.

30
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:28,180
There are many incorrect sentence endings because they have keywords similar to

31
00:03:28,180 --> 00:03:33,280
identified information areas, but the content of the complete sentences doesn't

32
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:34,980
match the information in the passage.

33
00:03:35,940 --> 00:03:40,840
Moreover, you need to remember that each sentence ending is used only once.

34
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:46,340
Finally, the questions might not come in
order as the information in the passage.

35
00:03:47,160 --> 00:03:52,380
Therefore, the information areas of possible
endings are placed randomly in the passage.

36
00:03:52,900 --> 00:03:57,480
So if there are additional question types whose questions follow the same order

37
00:03:57,480 --> 00:04:03,480
as the passage, you should focus on them first
before moving on to Matching Sentence Endings.

38
00:04:04,060 --> 00:04:08,160
Because at that time, we have a better understanding of the content in the

39
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:12,780
paragraphs, making it easier to scan the answers for Matching Sentence Endings.

40
00:04:13,260 --> 00:04:16,880
Because the questions might not come in order as the information in the

41
00:04:16,880 --> 00:04:22,360
passage, we can prioritize working on
questions containing unchangeable keywords.

42
00:04:22,960 --> 00:04:27,900
The reason is because these words are scanned easily in the passage, helping us

43
00:04:27,900 --> 00:04:32,960
save time and avoid focusing on difficult questions, thus missing easy ones.

44
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:38,960
As in the example, we can see that questions 19 and 20 are scanned easily in

45
00:04:38,960 --> 00:04:45,500
the passage because they have the unchangeable
keywords such as Heber Bosch or Pius Florus.

46
00:04:47,380 --> 00:04:50,420
So we've had a general introduction to this question type.

47
00:04:50,420 --> 00:04:52,280
Let's learn the steps to tackle it.

48
00:04:56,760 --> 00:05:02,020
Before introducing the steps, I want you to
answer the following interactive question.

49
00:05:04,300 --> 00:05:08,280
Let's see if we can come up with the same steps for Matching Sentence Endings.

50
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:15,440
Step 1. Read the requirement and underline
keywords in incomplete sentences and endings.

51
00:05:16,220 --> 00:05:23,880
Step 2. Apply scanning skills with keywords in incomplete
sentences to locate the information area of each question.

52
00:05:24,820 --> 00:05:29,600
Step 3. Compare endings with the information
area to choose the correct answer.

53
00:05:30,860 --> 00:05:34,380
Now we're going to practice each step to handle this reading passage.

54
00:05:39,980 --> 00:05:44,220
We're going to finish question 1 together and start with step 1

55
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:49,980
Read the requirement and underline keywords
in incomplete sentences and endings.

56
00:05:50,420 --> 00:05:52,920
We have 10 seconds to read the requirement.

57
00:05:57,350 --> 00:06:02,670
From the requirement, we can see that this is Matching Sentence Endings and the

58
00:06:02,670 --> 00:06:07,250
answers in the answer sheet will be the letters A to I.

59
00:06:07,970 --> 00:06:13,930
Underlining keywords in incomplete sentences and sentence endings gives us an

60
00:06:13,930 --> 00:06:16,870
overview of the content in each question.

61
00:06:17,670 --> 00:06:22,970
Underlining keywords in incomplete sentences helps us locate the information

62
00:06:22,970 --> 00:06:26,310
areas containing the answers effectively.

63
00:06:27,150 --> 00:06:33,230
Underlining keywords in sentence endings helps us understand and compare the

64
00:06:33,230 --> 00:06:39,110
endings which helps match sentence endings with incomplete sentences easily.

65
00:06:39,550 --> 00:06:42,830
So underlining keywords is very important, isn't it?

66
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:50,030
I'll give you 20 seconds to underline keywords
in the incomplete sentences and sentence endings.

67
00:06:50,550 --> 00:06:51,610
Start now!

68
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:16,140
This is my suggested answer.

69
00:07:16,480 --> 00:07:20,320
After underlining keywords, we're going to continue with the next step.

70
00:07:21,760 --> 00:07:28,440
Step 2. Apply scanning skills with keywords
in incomplete sentences to locate the

71
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:30,820
information area of each question.

72
00:07:31,420 --> 00:07:35,220
Why should we only scan the incomplete sentences?

73
00:07:35,620 --> 00:07:42,820
The reason is that the number of incomplete sentences
is less than the number of sentence endings.

74
00:07:43,140 --> 00:07:47,600
If we scan the sentence endings, it will be more time consuming.

75
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:50,620
Let's take a closer look at question 1.

76
00:07:50,980 --> 00:07:56,000
We can find our keywords in the first sentence of the second paragraph.

77
00:07:56,400 --> 00:08:02,000
The passage mentions communities dependent on agriculture or fishing.

78
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:08,100
In academic English, agriculture and fishing are known as primary industries.

79
00:08:08,480 --> 00:08:14,080
The text explains that these communities often find new sources of income

80
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:16,780
through tourism-related activities.

81
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:19,980
This tells us exactly where our answer is.

82
00:08:20,240 --> 00:08:21,840
Now let's move to the final step.

83
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:28,800
Step 3. Compare endings with the information
area to choose the correct answer.

84
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:33,919
After reading the first sentence of the information area, we have the following

85
00:08:33,919 --> 00:08:36,320
information taken away from it.

86
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:42,580
Communities that were once dependent on agriculture or fishing often find new

87
00:08:42,580 --> 00:08:46,600
sources of income through tourism-related activities.

88
00:08:47,060 --> 00:08:52,860
Younger generations see more profitable opportunities in the tourism sector.

89
00:08:53,300 --> 00:08:58,620
After understanding the main idea of the information area, we look at the

90
00:08:58,620 --> 00:09:01,980
sentence endings and see ending C.

91
00:09:02,160 --> 00:09:09,300
We also notice that the phrase lucrative
work is similar to profitable opportunities.

92
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:14,420
When combining question 1 with ending C, we have this sentence.

93
00:09:15,260 --> 00:09:21,060
Communities traditionally reliant on primary industries often discover more

94
00:09:21,060 --> 00:09:23,460
lucrative work in the tourism industry.

95
00:09:24,260 --> 00:09:26,760
So C is the answer to question 1.

96
00:09:27,220 --> 00:09:30,240
So we've followed the steps to do question 1 together.

